throughout the state of Oregon.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:39.480
And so our program
licenses health care professionals who

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:40.930
are first responders,

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:44.400
including EMTs and paramedics
of all kinds, all shades.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.980
And we also regulate
ambulance services.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:52.100
We

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:54.770
have a data system for

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:57.970
electronic patient
care records for

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:03.540
field responses, so ambulance
calls that do patient care

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:04.650
or transport a patient.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.070
Every one of those patient
records is in a repository that

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:12.060
we get. That data comes
in practically real time.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.475
We have all those within
48 hours of an event.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:18.270
And then we also have several
time-sensitive emergency

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:21.540
registries that we
manage, including trauma,

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:23.115
and cardiac arrest, and stroke.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.765
Some of those have
integrations with the EMS data.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:33.495
And one of the things I'm really
excited about getting to work on is

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:38.100
a project with the Oregon
Department of Transportation,

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.560
where we're looking at the
feasibility of interoperability

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:45.960
between the various EMS data sets
that we have and crash data.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:47.760
And so that--

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.140
that's really exciting too.

00:03:49.750 --> 00:03:51.510
And then outside of work,

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:57.645
I do play a couple of
musical instruments from time to time.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.320
That's a topic that comes
up often in this space.

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.300
