move into a dorm at the University

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:12.345
of Oregon in Eugene. And we
just got to take him there for

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:17.399
a couple of orientation sessions.
And I'm excited about that.

00:04:17.399 --> 00:04:20.160
He's sort of interested in
data science, which I'm,

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:23.445
you know, crossing my
fingers, but also like,

00:04:23.445 --> 00:04:25.300
go have fun and
have an adventure.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.810
And then I also
have a two-year-old,

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.250
or soon to be two-year-old. So

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:33.080
it's daycare and college
at the same time for me. So

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:38.230
I'm happy to have a
permanent government position

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:40.710
at this time so.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:43.110
- You are busy.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:45.105
- I'm very busy, yes. Thanks.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:49.060
- Well, thank you so much,
Andey, for joining us.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:53.230
I was thinking back to when we
first got to chat one on one.

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:56.610
And I think it was because
you were building up your own

00:04:56.610 --> 00:05:00.990
community internally when we first
got to chat one on one.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:04.620
So I'd love to learn more
about that experience and

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.440
what led you to start
an internal group,

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.770
as we're waiting for questions
to come in from everybody here.

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:12.420
- Yeah, so--

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:12.990
and I'm

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:14.905
correcting a couple of

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:18.570
acronyms. If folks--

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:19.920
if I start peppering
the acronyms,

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:23.280
I'll try to throw those
in the chat too. So yeah,

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:25.725
our community group
really arose out of

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:29.100
a couple of sections
got together,

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:32.815
the Injury and Violence
Prevention section and the EMS

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:35.145
folks in my program.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:39.870
And we wanted to
do some training in R,

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:44.970
relating to kind of upskilling the
team to be able to collaborate.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:45.470
And--

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:48.270
and really,

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:51.240
we were working on a
procurement for Posit for

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.512
enterprise solutions so
that we can use Connect

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.270
and do more with the
data that we have.

00:05:58.270 --> 00:05:59.010
And so

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:00.735
out of this training,

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:06.480
we ended up creating a
Teams group. So our whole organization,

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:09.030
our primary way of
communicating with each other

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:11.850
is email through Outlook
and then chat through Teams.

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:15.670
And we have lots of
channels that support various working groups.

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:18.750
And so there was a Team channel
that was created for that

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:20.055
RStudio Academy cohort.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:22.160
And

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:25.500
after the 10 weeks
of us together,

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:28.830
we all felt like we just wanted
to share everything we had

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:32.640
learned with everyone else. And so
we opened up the group to

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.005
everybody. And

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:36.810
the first week, we had--

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:37.500
there was--

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:38.880
so there was eight
of us to start with.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:42.510
We had about six people join
in that first week when we

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:43.330
finished and said,

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:45.795
let's just invite everyone and
let's share what we've learned.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:51.470
The next month, there was
about 40 people in that group.

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:54.970
And by the second month, we had over
100 people in that group. And that was--

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:56.875
and that's only word of mouth.

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:59.905
There isn't anyone running
the group right now.

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:01.510
We have--

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:05.120
it's all sort of like,
we just get together and

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:09.130
what do people want? What
do people need? What can folks

00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:11.395
bring? Who can we find
to help bring that?

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:14.860
And so it's fairly organic.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:18.790
And we're doing a little
bit of work now to try to

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:21.130
make it a little more formal,

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:23.080
but it's still very
much a work in progress.

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:26.950
- That's great. So it all
started with those people who were--

00:07:26.950 --> 00:07:27.520
- The Academy.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:27.790
- [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:28.135
together?

00:07:28.135 --> 00:07:28.810
- Yeah,

00:07:29.470 --> 00:07:30.400
the academy cohort.

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:30.670
-

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:33.580
I figured it probably would
be helpful for anybody who hasn't

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:38.050
heard of what Academy is
to just explain what that process

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:39.535
was the team went through too.

00:07:40.225 --> 00:07:41.050
- Sure.

00:07:41.950 --> 00:07:43.420
So we

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:48.310
learned about it
through the website,

00:07:48.310 --> 00:07:50.540
and then got in touch with

00:07:51.610 --> 00:07:55.810
the folks at Posit, and
learned that it's 10 weeks,

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:57.700
where we get a--

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:00.955
where our group is assigned
a mentor and we get

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:03.520
some health care
data to work with.

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:07.840
And we go through
some tutorial modules.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:09.190
Some of this is asynchronous,

00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:11.050
some of this we could
choose to work together.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:14.560
It was really up to the
group how to manage getting through

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:15.910
the tutorial materials.

00:08:15.910 --> 00:08:17.540
And then we would do

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:22.480
an exercise each week
where you reproduce

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:26.140
something, right,
either a table,

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:30.310
or a way to clean the
data and arrange it, or a visualization,

00:08:30.310 --> 00:08:34.210
and then from there, build
on it on your own, right, do

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:36.970
something to expand upon
that topic that week.

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:39.474
And so our group really

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:44.169
got a couple of things
out of that experience, sort of

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:48.160
drops the barriers and some
of the initial hesitation about

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:51.910
sharing code with one
another. And we learn how to

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:56.545
support each other in
improving code and how to--

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:00.310
it's not just about speaking,

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:04.490
you know, kindly
or sandwiching your

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:07.120
critique with some
positive things.

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:08.510
Like, it's more than that.

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:11.410
It's about understanding where each
other is in the learning

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:15.565
process and really helping
each other achieve our goals.

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:19.390
And so there's a lot of
learning how to listen to each

00:09:19.390 --> 00:09:21.010
other too. So that was really--

00:09:21.010 --> 00:09:22.600
I felt that was really--

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:24.220
a really great thing
to come away with.

00:09:25.072 --> 00:09:27.585
- That's awesome. I bet
there's something to

00:09:28.290 --> 00:09:30.390
learn from that experience too,

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:33.240
like you had this close knit
group of people who were

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:36.330
already working together who
helped build this community.

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:39.405
And that's probably helped
to make it successful too.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:41.760
I see--

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:43.080
oh, sorry. You go ahead, Andey.

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:46.120
- No, I was just going to
say and one of the references that

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:49.035
I've been using personally
in just sort of guiding

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:53.475
my role in this is a book by,

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:57.060
let's see, it's Josh Bacon or--

00:09:57.630 --> 00:09:59.340
I could grab it real quick.
It's called The

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:02.230
Art of
Community. And

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:04.830
the author is

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:09.210
someone from the Linux community.

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:12.990
And so a lot of the content
from this book and the approach

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:16.110
from this book has been kind
of folded into the Linux Foundation

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:17.900
culture.

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:23.820
And so anything that I've
ever done that touches Linux

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:25.860
Foundation work
has just felt so

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:31.680
intentionally inclusive and

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:37.350
just oriented in a way that
I feel really strongly to want to

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:38.330
align with it. And

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:42.090
so I've been doing a little
work on my own to try to

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:45.915
steer my participation
in that direction anyway.

00:10:45.915 --> 00:10:48.060
- That's great. Thank you.

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:50.790
I'm going to jump over to
some of the questions here in the

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:53.565
chat. And I love to
see the conversation happening too.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:58.655
Ted, I think you had
a question about the group.

00:10:58.655 --> 00:11:02.370
- Yeah. I mean, well, Andey and
I know each other. So hey, Andey.

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:03.210
- Hi, Ted.

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:03.420
-

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:07.500
So one of the things that
I've heard a lot with starting these

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:10.830
groups is people really
want to participate,

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:11.880
but they're so busy.

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:13.460
So

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:18.550
I found that one of the routes to
kind of improving that is to get sponsors

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:22.515
among leadership. Do you have
any experience with that?

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:27.270
- No. That's a really
interesting idea.

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:32.470
And I'm curious about what the
role of the sponsor really would be.

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:36.285
Because I would want to be
able to articulate that to--

00:11:36.285 --> 00:11:40.210
in I'm in my head it
sounds kind of like, mentoring, like,

00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:43.300
someone who's in a leadership
position that can say,

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:46.450
go ahead and participate
because we're getting this

00:11:46.450 --> 00:11:48.650
value back out of it, or
something along those lines.

00:11:48.650 --> 00:11:52.240
And then folks feel like they have
the space to ask for that, right?

00:11:52.240 --> 00:11:53.515
- Yeah. I think--

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:58.900
I think just someone who can
formally say that for an entire

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:01.675
group, that it's OK
to spend time on this.

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:05.440
I think also it's just people--

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:07.480
like, people who are willing--

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:07.840
like,

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:11.230
if your supervisor is willing for
you to dedicate time to it,

00:12:12.550 --> 00:12:14.980
to organizing,
things like that.

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:15.890
- Yeah.

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:18.130
I mean, right now,

00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:21.770
one of the things we're working
on is getting leadership.

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:22.990
And when I say leadership,

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:26.230
I mean the Oregon Health
Authority Director's Office.

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:29.875
Like, our overall arch
agency leadership is getting

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:35.260
some support there. We've got
a couple of advocates right now.

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:37.540
But when I mean
formalizing the support,

00:12:37.540 --> 00:12:40.570
like having something where
someone's got a project they're

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:43.810
working on and they're
spending time supporting this community.

00:12:44.650 --> 00:12:45.710
And so that's

00:12:46.690 --> 00:12:51.100
coming from not just our
folks in public health,

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:52.660
but that's coming from
all over the agency.

00:12:53.950 --> 00:12:54.450
So

00:12:56.030 --> 00:12:58.200
I think we're heading
in that direction.

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:01.050
But the idea of sponsors sounds
really interesting to me.

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:04.115
It's also similar to
where it kind of--

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:05.840
I guess it has a different

00:13:09.020 --> 00:13:12.150
