00:17:59.550 --> 00:18:00.420
ANDEY-NUNES BREWSTER: Hi.

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:03.210
- So like, big picture,
I'm super fascinated at how--

00:18:03.210 --> 00:18:07.185
like, I conceive of emergency services
as being, like, this whole other

00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:12.270
ring of service providers
in the medical landscape.

00:18:12.270 --> 00:18:16.950
And I'm curious how they
interact with you as an agency.

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:18.180
And then tactically,

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:21.810
how does how does their data
come into your landscape?

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:22.440
And then--

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:24.940
and I know I'm sort
of rolling on and on,

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:25.800
but I'm really curious, like,

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:27.960
what kinds of problems are
you solving with that data?

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:30.960
Is it about like,
resource allocation? Is it about--

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:33.300
is it about where people
are in terms of location,

00:18:33.300 --> 00:18:36.375
like distribution of services
and that kind of stuff?

00:18:36.930 --> 00:18:38.370
I realize there's
a bunch there.

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:40.770
Whatever is kind of interesting in
there that you want to speak

00:18:40.770 --> 00:18:41.910
to I'd love to hear about.

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:43.770
- Yeah, so--

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:44.270
so

00:18:45.030 --> 00:18:48.045
our program is the
regulatory body. We're the regulators.

00:18:48.045 --> 00:18:49.020
ALAN: OK.

00:18:49.020 --> 00:18:49.170
-

00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:51.570
Folks that provide these
services are licensed through

00:18:51.570 --> 00:18:52.890
our office. And

00:18:53.850 --> 00:18:57.360
there are standards of care
in place for providing those

00:18:57.360 --> 00:19:02.580
services. And we collect the data
for that to be able to do

00:19:02.580 --> 00:19:05.580
quality improvement and
to do compliance work.

00:19:06.330 --> 00:19:08.360
So we do compliance for

00:19:09.240 --> 00:19:10.050
ambulance services.

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:13.320
And part of that is
reviewing your records--

00:19:13.320 --> 00:19:17.985
and are you completing your
patient care records? Are you

00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:23.010
appropriately checking the
right boxes for particular

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:24.780
cases? So there's--

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:27.640
And then we also do that for
trauma hospitals as well.

00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:31.605
So certified trauma centers, that
data is used to verify

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:36.240
aspects of the patient care
itself and also verify aspects

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:38.970
of their performance
improvement because all

00:19:40.050 --> 00:19:43.500
trauma hospitals are required
to have an ongoing performance

00:19:43.500 --> 00:19:44.205
improvement program.

00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:45.900
So--

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:50.140
so essentially, the data is
used for compliance and safety,

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:53.020
but it is also used for--

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:58.292
in the field, it can be
used for decision making in

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:00.145
patient care.

00:20:00.890 --> 00:20:06.430
So one of the problems
that we've tackled recently is

00:20:06.430 --> 00:20:09.290
documentation of weight for

00:20:10.450 --> 00:20:14.260
patients who receive a
weight-based medication in the

00:20:14.260 --> 00:20:18.980
field. And so this
is especially important in pediatrics.

00:20:20.030 --> 00:20:20.530
And

00:20:22.280 --> 00:20:26.300
our program was able to collect
data to see when there's a

00:20:26.300 --> 00:20:29.870
weight-based medication that
is administered in the field,

00:20:29.870 --> 00:20:33.080
how often is the
weight documented in the record.

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:35.810
We developed a performance
metric out of this.

00:20:35.810 --> 00:20:39.560
And we did a letter
writing campaign to our ambulance

00:20:39.560 --> 00:20:41.285
agencies. And now,

00:20:43.310 --> 00:20:46.790
our expectation was that this
would be a pretty conventional

00:20:46.790 --> 00:20:49.760
thing. We'd see a 20%
response rate or something super low.

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:52.260
But it was off--

00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:53.190
it was off the chart.

00:20:53.190 --> 00:20:53.795
We went from

00:20:55.540 --> 00:21:00.376
somewhere in the, you know,
below our threshold to almost 100%

00:21:01.370 --> 00:21:05.330
within a couple of months
of implementing of that letter

00:21:05.330 --> 00:21:08.120
writing campaign and sharing
back with everyone their

00:21:08.120 --> 00:21:10.670
performance metric and where they
were compared to where we

00:21:10.670 --> 00:21:12.200
are as the state.

00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:14.810
And we're now doing multiple
performance metrics.

00:21:15.530 --> 00:21:17.960
And so we do them for
both EMS and trauma.

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:19.490
A lot of that is
on our website.

00:21:21.170 --> 00:21:21.670
So

00:21:22.490 --> 00:21:24.290
I hope I touched
on some of that.

00:21:24.290 --> 00:21:25.820
In terms of how the
data gets to us,

00:21:27.590 --> 00:21:29.300
we actually manage--

00:21:29.300 --> 00:21:31.900
we have third-party vendors who

00:21:32.660 --> 00:21:33.160
provide

00:21:34.520 --> 00:21:36.710
electronic patient
