00:21:38.450 --> 00:21:39.470
And we have--

00:21:39.470 --> 00:21:43.290
so we have a vendor
who provides a software for our

00:21:45.030 --> 00:21:48.172
electronic medical
record systems and--

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:52.950
and then also for our
licensing management system and then also

00:21:52.950 --> 00:21:56.040
for the trauma registry.
And the folks that do the

00:21:56.760 --> 00:21:59.760
EMS system also provide our
stroke and care registry

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:00.780
integrations as well.

00:22:01.780 --> 00:22:04.920
And so we do a lot of vendor
management and a lot of

00:22:06.120 --> 00:22:08.715
user support between
our users and

00:22:09.570 --> 00:22:11.010
things that we can
do with the vendor,

00:22:11.010 --> 00:22:14.040
but things we also have to ask
the vendor to do for us, you know?

00:22:14.040 --> 00:22:16.290
- Awesome, thank you. Yeah,
that touched on, like,

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:17.730
a whole bunch of stuff
I'm interested in.

00:22:17.730 --> 00:22:20.850
It sounds like it's a neat
landscape of complex systems.

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:24.810
And then your ability to
pull performance things out of that

00:22:24.810 --> 00:22:27.570
data and address it directly
I think is really cool.

00:22:27.570 --> 00:22:29.895
It's super neat to
think about. Thanks.

00:22:29.895 --> 00:22:34.390
- Yeah, and R is really
crucial to that whole workflow. Because

00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:36.210
without it--

00:22:36.210 --> 00:22:40.120
we're using the vendor's tools
to assess how those software

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:44.713
are performing, but that's
not always even possible.

00:22:45.460 --> 00:22:46.950
And so having a way to

00:22:47.650 --> 00:22:50.040
pull data from one table,

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:53.520
look at where things are
moving into a submissions table from

00:22:53.520 --> 00:22:55.905
the other side, look at what
was received on the other side--

00:22:57.330 --> 00:23:02.100
