00:48:26.960 --> 00:48:29.825
then how do you integrate
the data across states?

00:48:31.120 --> 00:48:35.056
- That's a really,
really great question. So

00:48:35.750 --> 00:48:39.830
yes, each state implements
the national state data--

00:48:39.830 --> 00:48:41.790
data standard in
the way that they

00:48:42.830 --> 00:48:47.510
want to do it. So California,
a lot of larger states

00:48:47.510 --> 00:48:50.280
that have a lot more
hospitals, trauma centers than

00:48:51.140 --> 00:48:54.380
Oregon does, they
tell everybody, OK,

00:48:54.380 --> 00:48:55.640
you're required
to do this data.

00:48:55.640 --> 00:48:57.390
You're required to submit
this data to the state.

00:48:57.390 --> 00:48:58.460
Here's the state repository.

00:48:58.460 --> 00:49:00.950
Go out and get your
software and send it to us

00:49:02.100 --> 00:49:03.600
because we're the
regulators, you know?

00:49:04.830 --> 00:49:09.150
And so that's really left
up to those organizations.

00:49:09.150 --> 00:49:11.340
They go and they
procure software.

00:49:12.240 --> 00:49:13.245
Those vendors

00:49:14.460 --> 00:49:15.344
set up

00:49:16.230 --> 00:49:18.910
data pipelines
between the databases,

00:49:21.420 --> 00:49:21.920
and

00:49:23.010 --> 00:49:26.370
then it works, right? I
mean, that's the idea.

00:49:27.300 --> 00:49:30.346
What ends up happening
is because of

00:49:32.580 --> 00:49:34.770
requirements for
data validation,

00:49:36.780 --> 00:49:38.250
which a lot of
this is built into

00:49:39.390 --> 00:49:40.410
that software,

00:49:41.940 --> 00:49:43.680
and requirements for

00:49:44.430 --> 00:49:46.000
certain hospitals--

00:49:47.430 --> 00:49:49.500
level 1's, level
2's, and level--

00:49:49.500 --> 00:49:51.195
there's four levels of trauma

00:49:52.020 --> 00:49:53.040
accreditation.

00:49:53.040 --> 00:49:55.530
And those are based on the
levels of trauma care that can

00:49:55.530 --> 00:49:56.940
